This moves the list's two clients off the jj maildir and onto
lab-mail: beastie, for Pat, and lab-claude, for the agent. It is the
work planned in "Configure notmuch to use lab-mail" [1], and it
follows the client design approved in "[PATCH v2]
jails/instances/lab-mail: Spec the list's mail hub" [2]. The design
is updated for the INBOX, Next, Later, and Archive folders, and for
flag sync on beastie. The spec text is in
jails/instances/lab-mail/spec.org, in the same commits as the code.

The patches come in phases, so you can stop after any of them:

- Support, patches 1-5. Nothing changes until a client uses it.
  beastie gets isync and msmtp. The notmuch package adds a lab-mail
  profile beside the default one, plus samples of ~/.mbsyncrc and
  ~/.msmtprc for pat@p7x.dev. lab-claude pulls all four folders into
  the same profile, and can post with git send-email.
- Check, patch 6. "just check" in dotfiles/notmuch is the test you
  asked for. It logs in to lab-mail, makes a dry run of the sync,
  checks the profile, and talks to msmtp without posting.
- Switch, patches 7-8. Emacs reads the profile and posts through
  msmtp, with an Fcc into sent. M i, M n, M l, and M a move the
  message or thread at point to INBOX, Next, Later, or Archive.
  lab-process-mail syncs before each pass and answers only approved
  senders. It logs the rest. Each reply is saved by Fcc, so the loop
  still reads the outcome from the index.
- Cleanup, patches 9-14. The jj maildir pieces go. The work revset no
  longer names mail@upstream, so the bookmark can be deleted safely.

Trying it before the switch, after patches 1-6:

On beastie, as Pat, after installing isync and msmtp:

    pass insert lab-mail/pat@p7x.dev
    mkdir -p ~/mail/pat@p7x.dev/sent/cur ~/mail/pat@p7x.dev/sent/new \
        ~/mail/pat@p7x.dev/sent/tmp
    cd dotfiles/notmuch && just install
    mbsync lab-mail && NOTMUCH_PROFILE=lab-mail notmuch new
    just check

Then read the list in Emacs after M-x setenv RET NOTMUCH_PROFILE RET
lab-mail. Posting from Emacs still goes to the jj maildir until
patch 7.

On lab-claude, after the jail's files are installed, as claude:

    mkdir -p ~/mail/claude@p7x.dev
    cd ~/lab/default.jj/dotfiles/notmuch && just install
    mbsync lab-mail && NOTMUCH_PROFILE=lab-mail notmuch new
    just check

I ran the lab-claude steps against the live lab-mail, in a scratch
home, and all 12 checks passed. With your sample and no pass(1)
entry, the login check fails as it should. The check fails on the
database until the first notmuch new.

The switch, in order:

1. Import the jj maildir as the README from v13 describes. That
   recipe files every old message in INBOX, so your inbox tag would
   then show every message from others that you have not answered.
   You may prefer to change the recipe's "Far :lab-mail:INBOX" to
   Archive. The agent is fine either way, because its inbox rule is
   the same as today's: mail from others that it has not answered.
2. Apply patches 7-8. Run just install in dotfiles/emacs on beastie,
   and in src/lab-process-mail on lab-claude.
3. Apply patches 9-14 whenever you like. After that, the mail
   bookmark on upstream and ~/lab/mail.jj on lab-claude (jj workspace
   forget mail) can go. lab-devel and lab-claude-devel still mount
   lab-submissions.git, so I left that repo alone.

Notes:

- The series is based on b/dev as you asked, but it needs nothing
  there except "[PATCH v13 0/8] jails/instances/lab-mail: Build the
  list's mail hub" [3].
- The keys are yours to change. M is free in the show, search, and
  tree maps. The stock "a" still removes the inbox tag, and the next
  notmuch new puts it back on a message that is in INBOX and not
  answered. Use M a to archive, or I can rebind "a".
- inbox means in INBOX and not yet answered by you, not just in
  INBOX. The agent cannot move mail, so for the agent, answering is
  the only way a message can leave the inbox. The spec's alternatives
  section records this.
- The freebsd-port skill on b/dev still posts with
  maildir-deliver.sh. That is a separate series, so I left it alone.
  It needs the git send-email form once both land.
- Tested: the lab-process-mail tests (19 pass), the tag hook and the
  Emacs move commands against a scratch maildir with flag sync on, a
  pull and the check against lab-mail as claude@p7x.dev, git
  send-email --dry-run, and git am of the series onto b/dev, which
  gives the same tree. Not tested: a real post, your two-way sync, or
  Emacs interactively. Emacs's install_and_load test fails in
  lab-claude, because markdown-mode is not installed here. It fails
  before init.el reaches notmuch.

